+## Cache Replacement Policy
+
+The worker replaces cached sheets with 2Q. Every cached sheet belongs to one of two queues:
+
+- **First-use queue** — sheets requested once since admission, ordered oldest first.
+- **Reused queue** — sheets requested at least twice, ordered least-recently-used.
+
+A newly admitted sheet enters the first-use queue. A request served from the cache promotes the sheet from the first-use queue to the reused queue, and refreshes its position within the reused queue. Promotion happens on the second request for a sheet, so a comparison set is protected from the screening that follows it while one-off sheets remain evictable.
+
+Eviction proceeds in a fixed order: the oldest sheet in the first-use queue is evicted first, and the reused queue gives up its least-recently-used sheet only when the first-use queue is empty. The first-use queue is additionally bounded to a share of the cache budget — initially a quarter of it, so 3 GiB of the 12 GiB budget — which keeps a long screening from growing the queue without limit: sheets admitted beyond that share evict first-use sheets to stay within the bound, and the rest of the budget holds the reused queue. The share is a tuned parameter of the worker, not a promise about which clips stay cached.
+
+Protection is a consequence of a second request, not something a caller can ask for. No node outside the worker requests pinning, and a sheet requested once and compared later is still first-use when a screening reaches it.
+
+## Decisions & Alternatives
+
+| Decision | Chosen | Alternatives Considered | Rationale |
+|----------|--------|------------------------|-----------|
+| Cache replacement policy | 2Q, with the first-use queue bounded to a quarter of the cache budget | LRU; frequency-based eviction with aging | LRU cannot distinguish a sheet being compared from a sheet a screening merely passed through, and it evicts the comparison set exactly during a long screening. Frequency-based eviction with aging protects repeated use but adds counters, aging work, and slow decay when editors move to another production. 2Q protects reused sheets with two queues and one tuned parameter, and needs no counters. See [cache eviction policy](decisions/cache-eviction-policy.md). |
+
+## Open Questions & Future Decisions
+
+### Resolved
+
+1. Cache replacement policy — 2Q, with the first-use queue bounded to a quarter of the cache budget.
+
+### Deferred
+
+1. The first-use share of the cache budget is a tuning parameter; its value is to be adjusted against measured workloads.
+2. A reused-queue sheet is evicted directly rather than demoted back into the first-use queue, so that a sheet a screening has moved past is not readmitted.
